From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <jschwender@posteo.net>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 05:08:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: rebuild the HTTP cache on RFC 9111 semantics and a
 streaming store

The HTTP cache kept every cached body as an anonymous `Vec<u8>` in the net
process, shared with the `Response` handed to script, and it decided freshness
with hand-rolled logic that predates RFC 9111. This replaces both halves.

Semantics now come from `http-cache-semantics` 3.0 (`http_cache::policy`):
freshness, `Vary` matching, the revalidation request and the 304 merge. Servo
keeps what the crate does not implement and what belongs to the fetch layer:
`stale-while-revalidate` (RFC 5861), the fetch cache modes, invalidation after
unsafe methods, and range requests. The cache is configured as a private
single-user cache, so `private` responses and responses to requests carrying
`Authorization` become storable, `s-maxage` is ignored, and `must-revalidate`,
`immutable`, `max-stale`/`min-fresh` and Date-based `Age` now take effect.

Three places compensate for the crate, each one a case where following it
literally is observably wrong. All three are pinned by web-platform tests, and a
crate upgrade can make any of them redundant or wrong:

  * It implements RFC 9111 4.3.4 literally and refuses to freshen a stored
    response when the 304 echoes no validator, even though the conditional
    request came from that very entry. Chromium and Firefox freshen;
    `policy::validator_for_revalidation` copies the stored validator into the
    304 so the crate's own merge applies.
  * Its 304 merge walks the *stored* header fields and calls `get` on the 304
    for each, so a field the 304 introduces is dropped and a field it repeats
    is truncated to its first value. RFC 9111 4.3.4 updates the stored fields
    with those from the 304, and browsers add new ones, so
    `apply_revalidation_headers` replaces each field the 304 carries outright,
    with all of its values, and adds those the stored response lacks.
    (`conditional-get.any.js` signals through a header only present on the 304;
    `cors/304.htm` repeats `Access-Control-Expose-Headers`.)
  * It stores only the statuses RFC 9110 lists as heuristically cacheable and --
    less obviously -- computes a zero freshness lifetime for anything else, so
    overriding `is_storable` alone would store entries that are instantly stale.
    RFC 9111 also allows any other final status carrying explicit freshness
    information, which is what browsers do and what `status.any.js` checks. Such
    a response is handed to the crate under a status it understands, while the
    real status lives in `EntryMeta` and is what a hit is served with. 1xx and
    304 are excluded.

Two further departures from the letter of the specs, both matching browsers: a
request's `Cache-Control: no-store` prevents reuse of an already stored response,
which RFC 9111 says it does not and `cc-request.any.js` requires that it does;
and 206s are stored, with `range.rs` mapping a requested absolute range onto the
stored offsets through the entry's `Content-Range`. Only bytes really stored are
served, `select_variant` skips variants that cannot answer the request, so a
stored partial neither shadows the complete response nor is shadowed by it, and
an unsatisfiable range sends the request to the network without deleting the
entry it could not serve.

Storage moves behind `CacheStore` (`http_cache::store`), which streams bodies
in and out and never hands out a complete body:

  * `TeeBody` copies each frame into the store *below* the content decoder, so
    the stored body is exactly what came off the wire with its
    `Content-Encoding` intact, and the copy is a refcount clone rather than a
    second allocation. The writer's bounded queue back-pressures the network.
  * A cache hit opens the entry and streams it through `Decoder` into
    `Data::Payload`, chunk by chunk, instead of one blob.
  * `ResponseBody::Streamed` marks a body that was delivered to the consumer
    and is retained nowhere. `retains_whole_body` decides when a whole body is
    still needed inside net: subresource integrity has to digest it, and a
    preload carries no integrity metadata but may later be consumed by a fetch
    that has some.
  * The request headers the policy remembers are reduced before they reach it:
    `Cookie` and `Authorization` are dropped, or replaced by a digest when the
    response's `Vary` names them, since the policy is serialized into the entry
    and a stored credential would outlive the credential itself. `Set-Cookie` is
    not stored either. Chromium's `HttpVaryData` digests the same fields.
  * `http_cache::inflight` replaces the `quick_cache` placeholder guard, which
    had leaked into the interface as `CachedResourcesOrGuard`. A second fetch
    of an in-flight entry now waits for the writer to commit and then reads the
    committed entry; when the writer aborts it fetches itself, instead of
    having its own fetch aborted as before.
  * The per-entry cap belongs to the store, which is what knows its own budget;
    `network_http_cache_max_entry_size` now means 0 = derive it.

`MemoryStore` is the only backend in this patch: byte-bounded, LRU, used for
private browsing, for `--temporary-storage`, when no cache directory is
available, and by the tests. The sqlite disk cache is gone, and with it
`rusqlite`, `sea-query` and `sea-query-rusqlite` leave `components/net` --
which also restores the RDB-only OHOS configuration's ability to drop sqlite.

Prefs: `network_http_cache_size` (an entry count) and `network_http_disk_cache`
(a path) are replaced by `network_http_memory_cache_size` and
`network_http_cache_max_entry_size`, both in bytes;
`network_http_disk_cache_size` keeps its name and gains 0 = automatic.

One unrelated fix comes along because the streaming path makes it reachable
reliably: `apply_algorithm_to_response` hit an `unreachable!()` whenever the body
was not `Done`, which a null body status makes it. That was already reachable
from content -- `main_fetch` sets the body to `Empty` for a null body status
*before* the integrity check runs -- and only escaped notice because the network
path raced it back to `Done`. It now returns a digest that cannot match, so the
fetch rejects, which is what `response-null-body.any.js` expects.

`preload/prefetch-document.html`: "different-site document prefetch with
'as=document' should not be consumed" now fails, and the expectation joins its
two siblings, which already fail. The cause is not this cache being wrong but
this cache being effective: Servo has no HTTP cache partition (fetch step 8.23,
upstream #33616), so a fresh `max-age=3600` response fetched by a cross-site
prefetch is visible to the later different-site navigation and answers it,
making the prefetch look consumed. Confirmed by running the test with
`network_http_cache_disabled=true`: that subtest then passes and the sibling
"same origin document prefetch without 'as' should be consumed" fails instead,
which shows prefetch consumption in Servo is provided by the HTTP cache rather
than by a prefetch cache. `CacheKey` already reserves the partition slot, so
landing partitioning will fix all three siblings without a format bump.

Validation: (1) `./mach test-unit -p servo-net` -- 402 tests, all passing;
`tests/http_cache.rs` covers storability, `Vary` selection, freshness, the
stale-while-revalidate window, the partial-entry rules, `If-Range`, and that the
stored body stays encoded. The digest of a `Vary`-nominated credential is pinned
from both sides: the entry still answers the cookie it was stored for, and does
not answer another one.
(2) Full `./mach test-wpt --profile medium` over all 57380 tests: 57257 ran as
expected. Every one of the 131 tests with any unexpected result was re-run
serially on this branch and on base 461582edd07, and only two differences
survived both passes: `cors/304.htm`, fixed here, and the expectation above.
Everything else is contention (40% of the parallel failures vanish at
`--processes 1`) or reproduces identically on base, including
`fetch/stale-while-revalidate/stale-image.html` and
`resource-selection-currentSrc.html`, which fail 3/3 on base in isolation.
(3) `./mach check` warning-free across the workspace, `./mach test-tidy` clean.
(4) The shared test target in `tests/main.rs` now reassembles a `Streamed`
body from the delivered chunks, so the existing body assertions in
`tests/fetch.rs` and `tests/http_loader.rs` exercise the streaming path
end-to-end rather than reading an accumulated buffer.
(5) After a rebase, re-check `http_network_fetch`'s body handling and step 10
of HTTP-network-or-cache fetch in `http_loader.rs` -- both are touched here and
change upstream often -- and re-run `fetch/http-cache` and `cors/304.htm`, which
are what pin the three compensations above.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implementation and testing)
